Q. How does springboot know which beans to create?
Ans:
It decides which beans to create based on:

1. Component Scanning
- The main class is annotated with @SpringBootApplication which includes @ComponentScan
- Spring scans the package of the main class and all sub-packages

@SpringBootApplication
public class MyApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(MyApplication.class, args);
    }
}

With this classes are automatically instantiated and registered as beans.
@Component, @Service, @Repository, @Controller / @RestController, @Configuration
------------------------
2. Configuration + Bean method
Spring Boot also creates beans explicitly defined in configuration classes.


@Configuration
public class AppConfig {
    @Bean
    public MyService myService() {
        return new MyService();
    }
}

- Every method annotated with @Bean becomes a Spring bean.
- So it will create bean for MyService
------------------------
3. Auto Configuration
Spring Boot uses auto-configuration classes (based on spring-boot-autoconfigure) that:
- Check what libraries are present(class path detection)
- Check existing beans
- Apply conditional logic like
	@ConditionalOnClass(DataSource.class)
	@ConditionalOnMissingBean(DataSource.class)
	@ConditionalOnProperty

Example:
- If it sees spring-web: configures a web server
- If it sees spring-data-jpa: configures JPA beans

------------------------
4. Conditional bean creation
Spring Boot avoids duplicates using conditions:
e.g.
@ConfitionalOnMissingBean

Means: Create this bean only if the user has not already defined one

@ConditionalOnClass
@ConditionalOnMissingBean
@ConditionalOnProperty
@ConditionalOnWebApplication

@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "feature.enabled", havingValue = "true")
@Bean
public FeatureService featureService() {
    return new FeatureService();
}
- Bean is created only if the property is enabled.
