Q3. How do you disable a specific auto‑configuration?
Ans:
In Spring Boot, you can disable a specific auto‑configuration in three commonly used ways, depending on your use case.

1. Using exclude in @SpringBootApplication (Most Common)
You can excliude auto-configurations right at the main class.

@SpringBootApplication(
exclude = { DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class }

2. Using excludeName (If class not on classpath)
Useful when you don't want to import the class directly
@SpringBootApplication(excludeName = "org.springframerork.boot.......")


3. Using application.properties / application.yml
You can disable auto‑configuration via application.properties or application.yml.
spring.autoconfigure.exclude=\
org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.jdbc.DataSourceAutoConfiguration,\
org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.orm.jpa.HibernateJpaAutoConfiguration
